{
  "correction_rules": [
    "Persistent positive bias: model forecasts are consistently too high versus realized prices (typically by ~3–10 EUR, larger during selloffs). Apply a downward level shift to the whole path, with a bigger shift when recent returns are negative.",
    "Downtrend underreaction / lag: in falling regimes the model declines too slowly and keeps prices elevated. Increase the magnitude of negative drift when the last 5–10 day trend is down (e.g., amplify recent negative slope).",
    "Horizon-dependent error: bias grows with forecast horizon (errors widen after ~day 7–10). Use a horizon-increasing downward adjustment (small in week 1, larger in weeks 2–4).",
    "Over-optimistic rebound/mean reversion: model repeatedly predicts rebounds back toward prior highs that do not materialize. Reduce mean-reversion strength; cap rebound speed after drawdowns and keep forecasts closer to recent lows.",
    "Overshoot after local spikes: when history contains recent peaks, the model anchors to them and overprojects higher levels. Replace anchoring to max/upper range with anchoring to recent median/EMA; apply a stronger correction when current price is below its 10–20d average.",
    "Volatility miscalibration in selloffs: realized downside moves are larger than forecast. Add asymmetric volatility: widen downside scenarios / shift distribution lower when downside momentum is present."
  ]
}
